Le mardi 15 novembre 2016 13:43:40 UTC, selpa'i a =C3=A9crit :
>
> Adjectival participles.=20
>
> Lojban does not have them, but let's look at the known options for=20
> translating phrases such as "to be a young ex-president" and "to be an=20
> abandoned house". What these have in common is that they contain two=20
> tanru units, at least one of which wants to be preceded by a=20
> tense/aspect/modal. This is not grammatically possible, because=20
> tense/aspect/modal tags apply to entire selbri and not to tanru units,=20
> and they cannot appear in the middle of a tanru. The following=20
> work-arounds are known to be available:=20
>
> 1) BE: {citno jatna be ba'o ku}, {se cliva be ba'o ku zdadi'u}=20
> 2) POI'I: {citno poi'i [ke'a] ba'o jatna kei}, {poi'i [ke'a] ba'o se=20
> cliva kei zdadi'u}=20
> 3) ZEI: {citno ba'o-zei-jatna}, {ba'o-zei-selcliva zdadi'u}=20
> 4) lujvo, but this only works with a few tags=20
> 5) GU'E (and possibly {ke}): {gu'e citno gi ba'o jatna} (not allowed),=20
> {gu'e ba'o se cliva gi zdadi'u} (allowed)=20
> 6) ME LO: {citno me lo ba'o jatna}, {me lo ba'o se cliva poi [ke'a]=20
> zdadi'u} / {me lo ba'o se cliva me'u zdadi'u}=20
>
> Option 1 is ugly to me because of the change in word order and the {ku}.=
=20
> Option 2 is very flexible and very verbose and very complex (it=20
> increases the nesting by one level)=20
> Option 3 is not practical when the tanru unit consists of more than one=
=20
> brivla (e.g. {se jai broda} or {na'e se broda}) as {zei} would then have=
=20
> to be placed between all the words.=20
> Option 4 is not at all general.=20
> Option 5 has potential but requires forethought, and {gu'e} also can=20
> claims too much, since it merges all the places, not just the x1 (just=20
> like {je}).=20
> Option 6 needlessly involves a sumti, which seems backwards to me,=20
> because I believe that predicates should be the base case and sumti=20
> should be derived from them, and not vice versa.=20
>
> If tag + tanru_unit yielded a tanru_unit instead of a selbri, then=20
> {(citno (ba'o jatna))} would be a valid tanru, as would {(ba'o se cliva=
=20
> (zdadi'u))}. However, this would require a lot more {cu} everywhere,=20
> specifically anywhere TAG was used in place of {cu} to close all the=20
> preceding constructs.=20
>
> I am looking for practical solutions to this rather commonly encountered=
=20
> problem.=20
>
> ~~~mi'e la solpa'i=20
>


Option 2 is satisfactory to me, but you may want a simpler way for {ba'o=20
jatna} being a tanru unit. Then I would suggest adding a new cmavo to=20
selma'o JAI. I call it {jo'ai} here.
7) {jo'ai} in JAI: {citno jo'ai ba'o jatna}, {jo'ai ba'o se cliva zdadi'u}

{jo'ai} is in the grammatical nature of JAI, but the first place of the=20
resulting tanru unit does not change, differently from {jai}.=20

mi'e la guskant jo'au juxre glico=20

